    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d374-a6">

                <pb facs="0748" n="638" sameAs="frus:frus1890/pg638"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure E in inclosure
                    1.—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Certificate of Dr. Daniel L. Cirlos.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Consulting Office of Dr. Daniel L. Cirlos</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Ciudad Porfirio Diaz</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1890-04-22"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >April</hi> 22, 1890</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>The undersigned, medical surgeon, hereby certifies: That the American, Shadrack
                    White, bears scars of wounds apparently the result of bullets from some firearm
                    of small caliber.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>(1)</label>
                    <item>On the front and back of the left forearm there is a scar as if burnt,
                        some 12 centimetres in extent; apparently the skin had been a brased, and
                        possibly an insignificant portion of the flesh.</item>
                    <label>(2)</label>
                    <item>In the right hand a small scar in radial aspect of middle finger, on the
                        lower inside surface; another scar on the outside of same finger and of same
                        extent; a scar in the web between the middle and ring fingers; scar of wound
                        at the metacarpo-phalangeal articulation; another on the surface (inside)
                        opposite the third metacarpal bone of little finger; and still another in
                        the lower portion of the fifth metacarpal bone at the adduct muscle of
                        little finger; these scars ranging from the inside out and from front to
                        back.</item>
                </list>
                <p>In present conditions the wound involves partial disability of the three fingers;
                    the hand can not be used save with great difficulty; and the injury, in my
                    opinion, will be permanent.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Daniel L. Cirlos.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
